  <DefinedExecutionError>
    <Identifier>ModuleNotRespondingError</Identifier>
    <DisplayName>Module Not Responding Error</DisplayName>
    <Description>
      The module did not respond — it may be disconnected or powered off.

      Check that the module is connected to the Flex and powered on, then retry.
    </Description>
  </DefinedExecutionError>
  <DefinedExecutionError>
    <Identifier>ModuleOperationError</Identifier>
    <DisplayName>Module Operation Error</DisplayName>
    <Description>
      The module reported an error while carrying out the requested operation.

      The underlying driver/module message is preserved to aid recovery (e.g.
      re-seat labware, check the module&apos;s status indicators, or power-cycle it).
    </Description>
  </DefinedExecutionError>
  <DefinedExecutionError>
    <Identifier>RunOwnershipError</Identifier>
    <DisplayName>Run Ownership Error</DisplayName>
    <Description>
      Protocol Engine currently owns the Flex for a prepared or active run.

      Read status through the connector as usual, but do not issue an independent
      SiLA hardware command. Stop the Protocol Engine run, or add the operation at
      an explicit UNITELABS mutation checkpoint through the controlled run-mutation
      HTTP endpoint, then retry.
    </Description>
  </DefinedExecutionError>
  <DefinedExecutionError>
    <Identifier>HardwareBusyError</Identifier>
    <DisplayName>Hardware Busy Error</DisplayName>
    <Description>
      The shared Flex hardware stayed busy longer than the configured lock timeout.

      Another SiLA client, an HTTP/Protocol Engine operation, or a long-running module
      action currently holds the hardware. Wait for that operation to finish (watch the
      RobotStatusProvider Status property) and retry, or raise lock_timeout_s in the
      connector configuration.
    </Description>
  </DefinedExecutionError>
  <DefinedExecutionError>
    <Identifier>HardwareError</Identifier>
    <DisplayName>Hardware Error</DisplayName>
    <Description>
      The Flex hardware reported an error that has no more specific defined error.

      The message preserves the Opentrons error code, name, and detail so the cause
      can be diagnosed or reported to vendor support. Inspect the robot, resolve the
      reported condition, re-home, and retry.
    </Description>
  </DefinedExecutionError>
  <DefinedExecutionError>
    <Identifier>InvalidStackerConfigurationError</Identifier>
    <DisplayName>Invalid Stacker Configuration Error</DisplayName>
    <Description>
      A Stacker labware or LED setting is outside the supported hardware range.

      Correct the value using the range in the error message and retry. For labware,
      use the exact assembled height including any lid or adapter.
    </Description>
  </DefinedExecutionError>
  <DefinedExecutionError>
    <Identifier>StackerMovementOutOfRangeError</Identifier>
    <DisplayName>Stacker Movement Out Of Range Error</DisplayName>
    <Description>
      The requested maintenance move exceeds the travel of the selected Stacker axis.

      Use a non-negative distance no greater than the axis-specific limit reported in
      the error message. Prefer RetrieveLabware and StoreLabware for routine workflows.
    </Description>
  </DefinedExecutionError>
  <DefinedExecutionError>
    <Identifier>StackerNotReadyError</Identifier>
    <DisplayName>Stacker Not Ready Error</DisplayName>
    <Description>
      The Flex Stacker is not installed, initialized, or safely closed for this operation.

      Confirm the Stacker is mechanically installed, close its hopper door, clear any
      obstruction, home it if necessary, and retry.
    </Description>
  </DefinedExecutionError>
</Feature>
